     1	# QuickLook — macOS Quick Look for Omarchy: find any file, see it instantly
     2	
     3	**Pool id:** P4 (Claude) · **Tribunal rank:** #4, 199 pts, consensus (all 5 ballots) · **Kind:** overlay + service
     4	
     5	One hotkey opens a fuzzy finder; as you type, a live themed preview renders beside the results — images, syntax-highlighted code, PDFs, CSVs. Space pins the preview fullscreen, Enter opens in the default app. The most-missed macOS feature, native to Omarchy.
     6	
     7	## Why this wins
     8	
     9	- **Catalog gap**: Omni, Search Bar, and Obsidian Search find files *by name and open them elsewhere*. Downloads (realgbbb) previews nothing. Nothing in the 607 renders the file *in place*. "Quick Look for Linux" is a sentence every switcher understands, and DHH's audience is full of Mac switchers.
    10	- **Utility depth**: this becomes a daily-driver plugin, which judges reward over gimmicks.
    11	- **Demo-ability**: type three letters, a PDF appears already rendered; arrow down, a CSV becomes a table; arrow again, code in your theme's colors. Visceral.
    12	- **Win condition is preview quality, not search.** Omni and Search Bar already find files; QuickLook competes on rendering the file in place, instantly, beautifully. Scope decisions below all serve that.
    13	
    14	## Plugin kind & manifest
    15	
    16	```json
    17	{
    18	  "schemaVersion": 1,
    19	  "id": "io.github.chris.quicklook",
    20	  "name": "QuickLook",
    21	  "version": "1.0.0",
    22	  "author": "chris",
    23	  "license": "MIT",
    24	  "description": "Fuzzy-find any file and preview it instantly: images, code, PDFs, CSVs — Space to pin, Enter to open.",
    25	  "kinds": ["overlay", "service"],
    26	  "entryPoints": { "overlay": "Overlay.qml", "service": "Service.qml" }
    27	}
    28	```
    29	
    30	`Service.qml`: supervises the helper process and keeps the index warm. `Overlay.qml`: finder + preview surface.
    31	
    32	**Hotkey:** NOT Super+Space — that is the Omarchy menu. Default suggestion `SUPER+.` (alternates `SUPER+SHIFT+P`), shipped as a `bindings.lua` snippet with a first-run collision check, and printed in the README and demo GIF.
    33	
    34	## Architecture
    35	
    36	**Two halves, hard boundary:**
    37	
    38	1. **QML in the shell process (thin, never blocks):** overlay UI — search field, results list, preview pane. Renders only data handed to it: `Image` from files/textures, `TableView` for parsed CSV rows, rich text for highlighted code. No file parsing in the shell.
    39	2. **`quicklookd` helper (bundled Rust binary, spawned by Service.qml as a child `Process` — not a second Quickshell process):** owns everything dangerous:
    40	   - **Cold path (instant, default):** name search via `plocate` when present, else a fast bounded walk — usable results on first summon with *zero index*. The trigram/fuzzy index (`nucleo` crate — fzf-quality matching as a library) warms in the background and takes over transparently; the UI shows "indexing… 62%" honestly.
    41	   - **Watches:** bounded — top-N directories by recency (default 2k watch cap, well under `fs.inotify.max_user_watches`), plus a periodic mtime rescan for the long tail. Watch coverage is visible in settings; the README documents the sysctl for power users.
    42	   - **Preview workers:** render previews to files in `$XDG_CACHE_HOME/quicklook/` and return JSON descriptors. Hostile formats (PDF) render in *disposable subprocesses* with wall-clock and memory limits — a crash or hang kills the child, never the helper (and never the shell).
    43	   - **Backpressure:** monotonic request IDs; the shell drops stale responses; in-flight renders capped at 1 (+1 prefetch of the top hit). A fast typer can never queue unbounded work.
    44	   - **Protocol**: newline-delimited JSON over stdin/stdout (`{"q":"invo","id":41}` → `{"id":41,"results":[…]}`). Dead-simple, testable from the CLI alone.
    45	
    46	**Data flow:** keystroke → debounced 30 ms → query to helper → ranked paths → selection change → preview request → helper returns cached or freshly rendered artifact → QML displays. Helper crash → Service restarts it (max 3×), overlay shows name-only results meanwhile. Shell process can never block or crash from a hostile file.
    47	
    48	**Privacy scope:** the index stores paths + selection history under the user's home. Default excludes: `.ssh`, `.gnupg`, `.password-store`, keyring dirs, `*.env`-heavy directories, plus node_modules/target/.git. Indexed roots are shown in settings and stated in the README's first paragraph, along with disk use (index + preview cache, LRU-capped at 500 MB with GC on start *and* on threshold).
    49	
    50	Dev loop: clone `omarchy.clock --edit`, hot-reload QML; `cargo test` the helper independently; `omarchy plugin validate` + `qmllint` in CI. Ship prebuilt static helper binaries (x86_64 + aarch64) in the repo, checksummed — precedent: the Quran plugin ships Go binaries.
    51	
    52	## Core technical approach (per format)
    53	
    54	**v1.0 renderer set is frozen at five + fallback** (scope is the #1 tribunal concern — four reviewers converged on cutting breadth for depth):
    55	
    56	| Format | Renderer | Notes |
    57	|---|---|---|
    58	| Images (png/jpg/webp/svg/gif) | QML `Image`/`AnimatedImage` directly | Helper only downsamples >20 MP images first (`image` crate) to protect shell memory |
    59	| Code/text (~40 langs) | Helper: `syntect` → constrained `<font color>` span markup (QML rich text supports only a small HTML subset — no CSS classes) | Theme colors mapped from Omarchy palette; files capped at 200 KB highlighted, larger fall back to plain text labeled "large file"; golden-test several languages |
    60	| PDF | **`pdftoppm` (poppler) primary** — near-universal on Arch, subprocess-isolated by nature, zero build risk | Feature-detected at startup; absent → designed "install poppler" empty state. pdfium is v1.1 enhancement at most, never on the critical path |
    61	| CSV/TSV | Helper parses first 500 rows (`csv` crate) → JSON → `TableView` | Delimiter sniffing; big-file streaming |
    62	| Directories | Entry listing + total size | Trivial |
    63	| Binary/unknown | Hex head + `file`-style magic description (via `infer` crate) | Never blank — always show *something* |
    64	
    65	**Video is progressive enhancement, never load-bearing:** QtMultimedia presence *and* codec support are validated on the exact shell build day 1; poster-frame extraction requires ffmpeg, which is feature-detected (not assumed) — if neither works, video rows show clean file metadata, never a broken player. The demo leads with PDF + image + CSV so a missing codec cannot blank it.
    66	
    67	**Cut to v1.1 (stated in README as roadmap):** markdown rendering, archive listing, video playback polish. Nothing half-works on a judge's machine.
    68	
    69	**Fuzzy quality is the product.** `nucleo` gives fzf-grade matching with smart-case, path-segment bonuses; boost by mtime recency and frecency from selection history in sqlite (atime is not used — relatime makes it a lie).
    70	
    71	**First-run demo corpus:** the plugin ships five tiny sample files (invoice PDF, photo, 5k-row CSV, themed `.rs`, a README) surfaced as the default view before the user types — the overlay is viscerally useful in the first second, even while the home walk warms.
    72	
    73	**Honest cut:** "press Space on a file selected in your file manager" (true macOS behavior) requires per-app selection introspection that Wayland doesn't offer generically. 1.0 is finder-first; a `quicklook <path>` CLI (`omarchy-shell shell summon … '{"path":"…"}'`) lets terminal users and file-manager custom actions invoke previews directly — that's the credible bridge, documented in README. `Enter` opens via `gio open` (portal-correct under Omarchy's session), not bare `xdg-open`.
    74	
    75	## Risks & mitigations
    76	
    77	1. **Scope blowout** (the tribunal's top concern) → renderer set frozen at five + fallback above; markdown/archives/video-polish cut to v1.1; the recovered days go to the prefetch path until first-preview is <100 ms perceived.
    78	2. **PDF pipeline** → poppler-primary removes the pdfium static-musl build from the critical path entirely; subprocess isolation comes free; capability badge when poppler is absent.
    79	3. **Indexing ~/ storms inotify or stalls cold-start** → plocate/bounded-walk cold path serves instant results with zero index; watch cap + periodic rescan; "indexing…" shown honestly; hard limits (500k files, ignore lists).
    80	4. **Hostile/corrupt files crash or hang a renderer** → disposable subprocess with rlimits + wall-clock kill for PDF; `catch_unwind` + 2 s timeout for in-process Rust parsers; fuzz the top parsers with garbage inputs in CI.
    81	5. **Prebuilt binary distrust / arch mismatch** → ship x86_64 + aarch64 static musl builds of `quicklookd` (pure Rust, no pdfium — musl is now easy), `build.sh` for source builds, checksums in README.
    82	
    83	## 5-day build plan (agent fleet)
    84	
    85	- **Day 1 — de-risk:** spike QtMultimedia + ffmpeg detection on stock Omarchy (decides whether video appears at all); verify `pdftoppm` render path + subprocess limits; scaffold helper with plocate cold path + index warm-up working end-to-end from CLI. Parallel: repo/manifest/CI scaffold + hotkey collision check.
    86	- **Day 2 — vertical slice:** overlay UI (agent 1: search field, results, keyboard model); helper protocol with request IDs/backpressure + image/text/code renderers (agent 2); Service supervision + crash-restart (agent 3). Evening: type→see-image works.
    87	- **Day 3 — remaining formats + speed:** PDF, CSV, hex fallback, directories; frecency ranking; prefetch-top-hit path tuned until first preview <100 ms perceived (agent dedicated to latency alone).
    88	- **Day 4 — polish + hardening:** pinned fullscreen mode (Space), open-with (Enter / Ctrl+Enter reveals in file manager), theme-mapped syntax palette, demo corpus, animations, fuzz CI, README/GIF/preview.png. Fleet runs the judge-machine matrix.
    89	- **Day 5 — freeze:** dogfood, fix, tag, submit.
    90	
    91	## 60-second demo script
    92	
    93	1. (0–8 s) "Linux has never had Quick Look. Omarchy does now." `SUPER+.` — the demo corpus is already visible. Type `inv` — invoice.pdf renders *already*, page one crisp.
    94	2. (8–20 s) Arrow down through results: a 40k-row CSV appears as a clean table; a Rust file shows highlighted in the active theme's colors; a photo fills the pane.
    95	3. (20–35 s) Space — PDF pins fullscreen, j/k pages through it. Esc.
    96	4. (35–48 s) Switch Omarchy theme — reopen: chrome *and code highlighting* now match the new palette.
    97	5. (48–60 s) "Everything rendered locally, indexed live, nothing leaves the machine. Enter opens it; Esc and it's gone. QuickLook."
    98	
    99	(If the day-1 spike proved video on this machine, a muted webm plays in beat 2 — enhancement, never a dependency.)
   100	
   101	## Theme integration & polish checklist
   102	
   103	- [ ] All chrome from theme tokens; syntect theme generated from the Omarchy palette (bg, fg, accent → keyword/string/comment scales) and regenerated on theme change.
   104	- [ ] Zebra rows in CSV view from theme surface colors; PDF page shadow; image checkerboard for alpha.
   105	- [ ] First-result preview begins rendering *while typing* (prefetch top hit) — perceived instant.
   106	- [ ] Empty states designed: "indexing… 62%", "no matches", "can't render this — hex view", "install poppler for PDF previews" all styled, never blank panes.
   107	- [ ] First-run note states indexed roots + disk budget in one sentence.
   108	- [ ] Keyboard map printed in a footer hint bar (↑↓ navigate · Space pin · Enter open · Esc close).
   109	- [ ] README with GIF, install one-liner, `bindings.lua` snippet (`SUPER+.`), binary checksums, v1.1 roadmap (markdown, archives, video polish).
   110	
   111	## Testing plan
   112	
   113	- **Helper unit tests:** matcher ranking snapshots; every renderer against a corpus of 200 real files + 50 fuzzed/corrupt ones; render-timeout and subprocess-kill enforcement; stale-response drop.
   114	- **Protocol golden tests:** JSON descriptors stable across versions; request-ID ordering under out-of-order completion.
   115	- **Integration matrix (fleet on VMs):** fresh Omarchy; small home (5k files) and huge home (400k files — cold summon must still return plocate results <2 s); no-poppler machine; no-video-codec machine; aarch64; helper killed mid-query (restart transparent); 8-hour soak with watch churn — shell RSS flat.
   116	- **Cold-judge test:** `omarchy plugin add <repo> --enable`, first summon <2 s to usable results with zero config (demo corpus + plocate cold path make this true even on a cold machine).
   117	- **Performance budgets enforced in CI:** query <10 ms warm, image preview <100 ms, PDF first page <300 ms, code highlight <150 ms at 200 KB cap, shell-side allocation per preview <30 MB.
   118	
   119	## Tribunal review
   120	
   121	**Verdicts:** Claude Fable 5: STRONG · Grok 4.6: SOLID · GPT-5.6 Sol: NEEDS-WORK · Kimi K3: NEEDS-WORK · DeepSeek V4 Flash: STRONG
   122	
   123	**Applied:**
   124	- **Scope frozen** to five renderers + hex fallback (images, code, PDF, CSV, directories); markdown, archives, and video polish cut to a stated v1.1 roadmap — the two NEEDS-WORK verdicts were both about breadth, and the recovered time goes to sub-100 ms perceived preview latency (GPT, Kimi, Grok).
   125	- **PDF backend flipped to poppler `pdftoppm` as primary**, pdfium removed from the critical path entirely — 4/5 reviewers independently flagged the static-musl pdfium build as the single biggest install risk (Grok, GPT, Kimi, DeepSeek).
   126	- **Hotkey changed off Super+Space** (it's the Omarchy menu) to `SUPER+.` with a Lua bindings snippet and first-run collision check (Grok).
   127	- Hostile-format rendering moved to **disposable subprocesses with rlimits** (free with poppler) rather than relying on `catch_unwind` (GPT).
   128	- **Backpressure protocol**: monotonic request IDs, stale-response drop, in-flight cap 1+1 prefetch (Kimi).
   129	- **Cold-start honesty**: plocate/bounded-walk is the default cold path with instant results; the trigram index warms in background; watch cap + periodic rescan replace naive recursive inotify (Kimi, DeepSeek, GPT).
   130	- Video demoted to detect-both-QtMultimedia-and-ffmpeg progressive enhancement with a metadata-only fallback; demo script no longer depends on it (Grok, Kimi, GPT).
   131	- Code highlighting emits a constrained `<font color>` span set with a 200 KB cap and golden tests (all five reviewers flagged the QML rich-text subset).
   132	- Privacy: default exclude list for secrets dirs, indexed roots visible in settings and README (DeepSeek).
   133	- Frecency drops atime (relatime); `Enter` opens via `gio open` (Claude, DeepSeek).
   134	- First-run **demo corpus** of five sample files as the pre-typing default view (Grok).
   135	
   136	**Rejected:**
   137	- Claude's "preselect the most recent Downloads file after a download" — charming but new surface area (download detection) in a spec whose verdicts demanded cuts; v1.1.
   138	- Grok's "render code to a cached PNG/Pango image in the helper" — constrained-span rich text is simpler, selectable, and theme-recolorable without re-render; the cap + golden tests address the actual risk.
   139	- Kimi's zip-bomb entry cap — moot, archives are cut from v1.0.
     1	# Omarchy shell
     2	
     3	`omarchy-shell` is a single long-running [Quickshell](https://quickshell.org/)
     4	instance that hosts the Omarchy desktop. Hyprland autostart launches one shell
     5	per graphical session; everything else — the bar, background switcher, panels,
     6	and overlays — runs **inside** the shell as a plugin.
     7	
     8	Hosting everything inside one shell means:
     9	
    10	- shared services and singletons live once, not once per process
    11	- summoning a panel is an IPC call into a process that is already running,
    12	  not a fresh `quickshell -p ...` cold start
    13	- third-party plugins can be loaded from disk without changing any source
    14	  code in Omarchy itself
    15	
    16	The runtime layout:
    17	
    18	```
    19	shell/
    20	  shell.qml              entry point (ShellRoot)
    21	  services/
    22	    PluginRegistry.qml   discovers, validates plugins, looks up enabled state in shell.json
    23	    BarWidgetRegistry.qml unified registry for bar widgets (1p + 3p)
    24	  plugins/
    25	    bar/                 first-party plugins (see plugins/README.md)
    26	    image-picker/
    27	    menu/
    28	    notifications/
    29	    panels/
    30	      audio/
    31	      bluetooth/
    32	      monitor/
    33	      network/
    34	      power/
    35	      weather/
    36	    agents/
    37	    services/
    38	      battery/
    39	      idle/
    40	    osd/
    41	    polkit/
    42	```
    43	
    44	The plugin discovery path is documented in [plugins/README.md](plugins/README.md).
    45	
    46	## Plugin manifest
    47	
    48	Every plugin ships a `manifest.json` describing what it is and how the
    49	shell should load it. Minimal example:
    50	
    51	```json
    52	{
    53	  "schemaVersion": 1,
    54	  "id": "my.org.cool-clock",
    55	  "name": "Cool clock",
    56	  "version": "1.0.0",
    57	  "author": "You",
    58	  "description": "A clock that does cool things",
    59	  "kinds": ["bar-widget"],
    60	  "entryPoints": { "barWidget": "Widget.qml" },
    61	  "barWidget": {
    62	    "displayName": "Cool clock",
    63	    "category": "Time",
    64	    "allowMultiple": false,
    65	    "defaultSection": "left",
    66	    "defaults": { "format": "HH:mm" },
    67	    "schema": [
    68	      { "key": "format", "type": "string", "label": "Format" }
    69	    ]
    70	  }
    71	}
    72	```
    73	
    74	Supported `kinds`:
    75	
    76	| Kind         | What it is                                                   |
    77	|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|
    78	| `bar-widget` | A component that the active bar can drop into a section      |
    79	| `panel`      | A persistent or summoned floating window (e.g. OSD)          |
    80	| `overlay`    | A fullscreen overlay (e.g. background switcher)              |
    81	| `menu`       | A summoned menu surface                                      |
    82	| `service`    | A headless singleton, no UI                                  |
    83	| `bar`        | A full bar option that can replace the built-in `omarchy.bar` |
    84	
    85	Only one `bar` plugin is active at a time. Missing or invalid selections fall
    86	back to the built-in `omarchy.bar`, so users always have a safe path home.
    87	Panels, overlays, and menus are loaded when summoned. Plugins that need
    88	to outlive a single summon can set `keepLoaded: true` (e.g. the image
    89	picker keeps its overlay window mounted between summons). First-party
    90	services are loaded at startup.
    91	
    92	The full schema lives in `services/PluginRegistry.qml`.
    93	
    94	## Installing a third-party plugin
    95	
    96	A plugin is a **git repo** with a `manifest.json` at its root. Adding one
    97	clones it straight into `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/<id>/` (named by the
    98	manifest id); updating is a fast-forward pull of that checkout.
    99	
   100	```bash
   101	omarchy plugin add https://github.com/acme/omarchy-weather.git
   102	omarchy plugin update acme.weather       # fetches, shows a diff, fast-forwards
   103	omarchy plugin update                    # updates every git-managed plugin
   104	omarchy plugin remove acme.weather
   105	```
   106	
   107	> ⚠️ **Plugins run as unsandboxed code inside `omarchy-shell`.** Adding warns
   108	> you before cloning, plugins land disabled so you can review the code before
   109	> enabling, and updates show a diff of the changes before touching anything.
   110	> Only add repos whose code you are willing to run.
   111	
   112	Each command is **interactive** when run bare in a terminal (gum pickers,
   113	confirmation, a diff to review) and fully **non-interactive** when given
   114	arguments. Pass `--yes` to skip every prompt — this is the path for scripts and
   115	AI agents:
   116	
   117	```bash
   118	omarchy plugin add https://github.com/acme/omarchy-weather.git --enable --yes
   119	omarchy plugin update --yes
   120	```
   121	
   122	The installer never runs plugin code, install hooks, or sudo — it only clones
   123	files, validates the manifest, and toggles enabled state over shell IPC. Since
   124	an installed plugin is a plain git checkout, anything beyond add/update
   125	(pinning a ref, switching branches) is ordinary git in the plugin directory.
   126	
   127	### Installing by hand
   128	
   129	You can still drop a plugin in without git:
   130	
   131	1. Put it in `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/<plugin-id>/` with a `manifest.json`
   132	   plus the QML referenced from its `entryPoints`.
   133	2. `omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins`.
   134	3. `omarchy plugin enable <id>`. Bar widgets start in
   135	   `barWidget.defaultSection`, or in the center when it is omitted, and can be
   136	   moved with `omarchy bar move`; a full bar replaces the one in use.
   137	
   138	The lower-level IPC equivalents remain available via `omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins`,
   139	`omarchy-shell shell enablePlugin <id> '{}'`, and `omarchy-shell shell listPlugins`.
   140	The `omarchy plugin` commands wrap those calls. `omarchy bar move` and
   141	`omarchy bar set` edit the persisted widget layout in `shell.json`.
   142	
   143	To hack on a built-in plugin safely, clone it into user config instead of
   144	editing the built-in source. The complete plugin directory is copied, including
   145	every declared kind and local dependency. A built-in id such as
   146	`omarchy.clock` becomes `<username>.clock` (e.g. `dhh.clock`), with `My Clock`
   147	as its display name. The username prefix keeps shared clones from colliding
   148	with each other or with other plugin authors.
   149	
   150	```bash
   151	omarchy plugin clone omarchy.clock
   152	```
   153	
   154	Cloning switches from the built-in to the new personal plugin, preserving an
   155	existing bar widget's position and settings. Setup > Plugins > Clone provides
   156	the interactive picker, then opens the new `<username>.*` directory in `$EDITOR`.
   157	Existing shortcuts and shell IPC calls made to the built-in id are routed to
   158	the enabled clone, so cloning does not require changing its callers. Removing
   159	an active clone switches back to its built-in source.
   160	Saving a file anywhere under `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/` reloads plugin code
   161	automatically; `omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins` remains available to force a reload.
   162	
   163	First-party plugins under `shell/plugins/` are discovered the same way and load
   164	by default. Disabling a non-widget records it in `disabledPlugins[]`; disabling
   165	a widget removes it from the bar layout while leaving its component available
   166	to add again. A full bar has no off state and is replaced by enabling another.
   167	
   168	## IPC contract
   169	
   170	The shell exposes a single `shell` IPC target plus whatever extra targets
   171	individual plugins register (e.g. the bar's `bar` target for refresh
   172	hooks, the image picker's `image-selector` target). `omarchy-menu` uses the
   173	shell target to summon the first-party `omarchy.menu` plugin instead of
   174	running a separate Quickshell instance.
   175	
   176	| Method                                   | Returns | Effect                                                |
   177	|------------------------------------------|---------|-------------------------------------------------------|
   178	| `ping`                                   | `ok`    | health check                                          |
   179	| `summon <id> <payloadJson>`              | `ok` / `unknown` | load + open a panel/overlay plugin           |
   180	| `hide <id>`                              | —       | close a previously-summoned plugin                    |
   181	| `toggle <id> <payloadJson>`              | —       | summon if closed, hide if open                        |
   182	| `call <id> <method> <arg>`               | string  | call a method on an already-loaded plugin             |
   183	| `rescanPlugins`                          | —       | re-walk plugin dirs and hot-reload plugin code        |
   184	| `reloadConfig`                           | `ok`    | reload `~/.config/omarchy/shell.json`                 |
   185	| `setPluginEnabled <id> <enabled>`        | `ok` / `unknown` | flip the persisted enabled bit (see note)    |
   186	| `listPlugins`                            | JSON    | every discovered plugin, sorted by name               |
   187	
   188	Direct invocation:
   189	
   190	```
   191	quickshell ipc -p $OMARCHY_PATH/shell call shell ping
   192	```
   193	
   194	Hyprland autostart launches the shell directly with `quickshell -p
   195	$OMARCHY_PATH/shell`. Use `omarchy-restart-shell` to stop every running
   196	instance of that config and launch one fresh shell process.
   197	
   198	A convenience wrapper, [`omarchy-shell`](../bin/omarchy-shell), forwards IPC
   199	calls to the running shell. It does not start the shell.
   200	
   201	```
   202	omarchy-shell shell ping
   203	omarchy-shell shell toggle omarchy.menu '{"menu":"root"}'
   204	omarchy-shell shell listPlugins
   205	omarchy-shell shell rescanPlugins
   206	```
   207	
   208	**Note on `setPluginEnabled`:** the `enabled` argument is a string. Only the
   209	literal `"true"` enables the plugin; every other value (including `"True"`,
   210	`"1"`, `"yes"`, or omitted) disables it. This keeps the IPC surface
   211	type-stable across QML's `string`-only IPC arguments.
   212	
   213	## Persisted state
   214	
   215	There is one user config file. Everything that distinguishes your
   216	customization from the shipped defaults lives in it.
   217	
   218	| Path                              | Owner          | Purpose                                                |
   219	|-----------------------------------|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------|
   220	| `~/.config/omarchy/shell.json`    | the shell      | full layout + per-entry settings + enabled plugin list |
   221	| `~/.config/omarchy/plugins/<id>/` | user           | drop-in third-party plugin source files                |
   222	
   223	The `config/omarchy/shell.json` default config describes the
   224	fresh-install state. When the user has no `shell.json`, the shell uses
   225	the defaults verbatim. Once the user customizes anything, `shell.json`
   226	becomes the authoritative file — we do **not** deep-merge defaults back in.
   227	
   228	### shell.json shape
   229	
   230	```json
   231	{
   232	  "version": 1,
   233	  "idle": {
   234	    "screensaver": 150,
   235	    "lock": 300
   236	  },
   237	  "bar": {
   238	    "id": "omarchy.bar",
   239	    "position": "top",
   240	    "transparent": false,
   241	    "centerAnchor": "omarchy.clock",
   242	    "layout": {
   243	      "left":   [ { "id": "omarchy.menu" }, { "id": "omarchy.workspaces" } ],
   244	      "center": [ { "id": "omarchy.clock", "format": "HH:mm" } ],
   245	      "right": [
   246	        { "id": "omarchy.audio" }
   247	      ]
   248	    }
   249	  },
   250	  "plugins": []
   251	}
   252	```
   253	
   254	### Storage rules
   255	
   256	1. **The active bar option is `bar.id`.** Omit it or set it to `omarchy.bar`
   257	   to use the built-in bar. Set it to another plugin id whose manifest declares
   258	   `kind: "bar"` to replace the full bar.
   259	2. **Every plugin instance is one entry.** Either in `bar.layout.<section>`
   260	   for bar widgets, or in `plugins[]` for panels, overlays, services,
   261	   menus, and anything else non-bar.
   262	3. **Settings are inline on the entry.** No `config:` sub-object, no
   263	   separate per-plugin settings file, no merge layers. The fields on each
   264	   entry are the values the plugin sees.
   265	4. **Built-in widget ids are namespaced.** Use ids such as `omarchy.clock`,
   266	   `omarchy.audio`, and `omarchy.network`. The migration rewrites older ids
   267	   like `Clock` and `AudioPanel` forward.
   268	5. **Third-party enabled ⇔ present.** A third-party plugin is enabled iff
   269	   its id appears somewhere in shell.json. For full bar options, that means
   270	   `bar.id`; for bar widgets, plugin enable/disable adds/removes layout entries;
   271	   other plugin kinds are enabled the same way. First-party non-bar plugins
   272	   are enabled unless listed in `disabledPlugins[]`.
   273	6. **Multiple instances** are allowed when a manifest sets
   274	   `allowMultiple: true`. Each instance is independent — e.g. two clock
   275	   widgets in different timezones are just two `{"id":"omarchy.clock", "timezone": ...}`
   276	   entries with their own values.
   277	7. **Idle timings are top-level.** `idle.screensaver` and `idle.lock`
   278	   are seconds since user idle began, so the default lock fires at 300s
   279	   even if the 150s screensaver starts first.
   280	8. **`version: 1` is required** at the top level. The shell will fall back
   281	   to defaults rather than load an unknown version.
   282	
   283	## Implementation history
   284	
   285	Built up in phases on this branch:
   286	
   287	- Phase 1 — `omarchy-shell phase 1: host the existing bar in a single shell`
   288	- Phase 2 — `omarchy-shell phase 2: plugin registry and bar widget registry`
   289	- Phase 3 — `omarchy-shell phase 3: fold bar-settings into the shell as a panel plugin`
   290	- Phase 4 — `omarchy-shell phase 4: absorb background-switcher as a plugin`
